{#- BGP peer-group, as zebra-rs `set` statements.
    Port of dockers/docker-fpm-frr/frr/bgpd/templates/general/peer-group.conf.j2.

    zebra-rs calls this a `neighbor-group`, and its shape differs from
    FRR's peer-group in one way that matters: the inbound/outbound route
    policy is a **group-level** `policy in|out` leaf, not a per-AFI one.
    FRR applies `route-map ... in` inside each `address-family` block, so
    a v4 and a v6 policy can differ on the same group. SONiC does not use
    that freedom here — PEER_V4 and PEER_V6 are separate groups, each
    with one family — so the flattening is lossless for these templates.
    It would not be for a group carrying both families.

    Paths verified against a running daemon; several plausible spellings
    are rejected (`afi-safi <af> policy in`, `allowas-in 1`,
    `soft-reconfiguration inbound`), and a rejected line is reported by
    `vtyctl apply` with exit 0, so guessing here fails quietly.

    Mapping, FRR -> zebra-rs:

      neighbor PG peer-group             -> neighbor-group PG
      neighbor PG route-map RM in        -> neighbor-group PG policy in RM
      neighbor PG route-map RM out       -> neighbor-group PG policy out RM
      neighbor PG allowas-in 1           -> neighbor-group PG allowas-in count 1
      (per-AF) neighbor PG activate      -> neighbor-group PG afi-safi <af> enabled true
      table-map RM (per-AF)              -> afi-safi <af> table-map RM

    GAP — soft-reconfiguration inbound. FRR's templates set it on both
    peer-groups unconditionally; zebra-rs has the capability internally
    (bgp/peer.rs) but exposes no config leaf for it, so it cannot be
    requested here. zebra-rs keeps received routes for its own soft-reset
    handling, so the operational effect is expected to be nil — but it is
    an unverified assumption, not a checked equivalence, and it belongs
    on the list of things to confirm before this replaces FRR in
    production. -#}
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V4
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V6
{#- allowas-in: ToR always, Leaf only when BBR is on. Same conditions as
    the FRR template, so the two stay in step if either is edited. -#}
{%- if CONFIG_DB__DEVICE_METADATA['localhost']['type'] == 'ToRRouter' %}
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V4 allowas-in count 1
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V6 allowas-in count 1
{%- elif CONFIG_DB__DEVICE_METADATA['localhost']['type'] == 'LeafRouter' %}
{%- if CONFIG_DB__BGP_BBR['status'] == 'enabled' %}
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V4 allowas-in count 1
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V6 allowas-in count 1
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V4 policy in FROM_BGP_PEER_V4
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V4 policy out TO_BGP_PEER_V4
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V4 afi-safi ipv4 enabled true
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V6 policy in FROM_BGP_PEER_V6
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V6 policy out TO_BGP_PEER_V6
set router bgp neighbor-group PEER_V6 afi-safi ipv6 enabled true
{#- Selective route download: an upstream line card / upper spine keeps
    the full table in BGP but installs only a filtered subset into the
    FIB. In zebra-rs this is a global per-AFI table-map, matching FRR's
    placement inside the address-family block. -#}
{%- if (CONFIG_DB__DEVICE_METADATA['localhost']['type'] == 'SpineRouter'
        and CONFIG_DB__DEVICE_METADATA['localhost']['subtype'] == 'UpstreamLC')
       or CONFIG_DB__DEVICE_METADATA['localhost']['type'] == 'UpperSpineRouter' %}
set router bgp afi-safi ipv4 table-map SELECTIVE_ROUTE_DOWNLOAD_V4
set router bgp afi-safi ipv6 table-map SELECTIVE_ROUTE_DOWNLOAD_V6
{%- endif %}
